<odoo>
    <data noupdate="1">
        <record id="group_mail_ingest_service" model="res.groups">
            <field name="name">Inbound Mail Ingest Service</field>
            <field name="comment">Internal system group for the SES-to-S3-to-Odoo inbound mail polling daemon. See docs/proposals/EMAIL_SEND_RECEIVE.md.</field>
        </record>

        <!-- ir.model.access.csv's access_res_partner_mail_ingest row (res.partner, read-only)
             exists because of a real production failure on 2026-09-27 (12 occurrences, every
             inbound mail to info@hams.com, hams1 odoo-server.log 15:35-15:40 UTC):
             "AccessError: Failed to write field mail.message.partner_ids / You are not allowed
             to access 'Contact' (res.partner) records", reported against THIS account's uid even
             though the failing message_post() ran under hams_helpdesk.user_helpdesk_service via
             zero_sudo's with_user() elevation. Root cause is Odoo 19 core, not this codebase's
             elevation chain (which was re-verified hop by hop and is correct):
             odoo/orm/fields_relational.py Many2many.write_real() calls _check_sudo_commands(),
             and for a comodel that sets _allow_sudo_commands = False (res.partner does, in
             base/models/res_partner.py) it re-runs the "read" access check on the linked
             partners as comodel.sudo(False).with_user(comodel.env.transaction.default_env.uid)
            , i.e. as the ORIGINAL request user of the whole transaction, which is whatever
             account made the RPC call (odoo/service/model.py sets transaction.default_env to
             the caller's env for every RPC; odoo/http.py does the same for HTTP requests).
             Every with_user()/sudo() applied later in the call chain is discarded for that one
             check. So when this daemon's account calls ingest_inbound_email(), any
             message_post(partner_ids=...) anywhere downstream (here: hams_helpdesk.ticket.
             _automated_routing_and_notification()'s assignment note) requires that THIS account
             can read res.partner. Reproduced exactly on a dev database by setting
             env.transaction.default_env to this account's env before calling
             ingest_inbound_email(); a plain `odoo shell` or a TransactionCase never reproduces
             it because there the transaction's default env is the superuser, which is also why
             two earlier "fixes" (an OdooBot grant, hams_open #473) changed nothing in
             production. Read-only is sufficient: the check is a read check on partners that
             already exist. See tests/test_mail_ingest.py's
             test_06_partner_ids_check_runs_as_the_request_user for the regression test that
             mirrors the real RPC dispatcher. -->
    </data>

    <data noupdate="0">
        <record id="user_mail_ingest_service" model="res.users">
            <field name="name">Inbound Mail Ingest Service</field>
            <field name="login">mail_ingest_service_internal</field>
            <field name="is_service_account" eval="True"/>
            <field name="active" eval="True"/>
            <field name="company_id" ref="base.main_company"/>
            <field name="company_ids" eval="[(4, ref('base.main_company'))]"/>
            <field name="notification_type">email</field>
            <field name="group_ids" eval="[(6, 0, [ref('group_mail_ingest_service')])]"/>
        </record>
    </data>
</odoo>
